WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 215: France: The Sun King: Louis XIV, the Hall of Mirrors, and the Man in the Iron Mask

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.920
[Rebecca] Today we enter the golden zenith of absolute monarchy.

4
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.960
[Rebecca] The court of Louis XIV, the Sun King.

5
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.440
[Rebecca] In the glittering halls of Versailles, France defined the art, diplomacy, and power of 17th century Europe.

6
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:28.080
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Louis XIV, the Hall of Mirrors, and the Man in the Iron Mask.

7
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:40.720
[George] Born in September 1638 after 23 years of childless marriage between King Louis XIII and Anne of Austria, the young prince was hailed across France as Louis de Eudenet, the god given.

8
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:50.320
[George] Ascending the throne at just four years old in 1643 under the regency of his mother and the shrewd Italian prime minister, Cardinal Mazarin.

9
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.360
[George] Louis's childhood was shattered by the traumatic civil wars known as the Fronde.

10
00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:08.240
[George] Rebellious Parisian magistrates and proud aristocratic princes, led by the great Conde, took up arms against the crown, firing cannon at royal troops, blockading Paris, and invading the royal palace.

11
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:18.640
[George] In January 1649, 10-year-old Louis was forced to flee Paris in the dead of winter on a freezing carriage ride to Saint Germain, sleeping on straw.

12
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:28.240
[George] In 1651, rebel mobs broke into the royal bedchamber in the Palais Royal, standing over the boy king's bed to verify he had not escaped.

13
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.280
[George] This terrifying humiliation seared an indelible lesson into the young king's mind.

14
00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:44.320
[George] He developed an unshakable hatred for Paris and an unyielding conviction that the unruly nobility must be stripped of independent military power and brought under absolute royal submission.

15
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:59.360
[George] The Fraunde Rebellion left Louis XIV with a permanent distrust of the Parisian mob and a burning resolve to strip the high nobility of independent provincial military governorships, ensuring that royal authority remained centralized exclusively in the crown.

16
00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:13.960
[George] The fronde had exposed the young king to the terrifying fickleness of the Parisian mob, instilling in Louis an unyielding conviction that absolute personal authority was the only salvation against the destructive chaos of feudal aristocratic rebellion.

17
00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:24.920
[George] When Cardinal Mazarin died in March 1661, the twenty two-year-old Louis XIV stunned his court and counsellors by declaring a revolutionary change in governance.

18
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:33.480
[George] Summoning his ministers, the young king announced: Up to this moment I have been pleased to entrust the government of my affairs to the late cardinal.

19
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.120
[George] It is now time that I govern them myself.

20
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.320
[George] You will assist me with your counsels when I ask for them.

21
00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:44.440
[George] I order you not to sign anything, not even a passport, without my command.

22
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:51.400
[George] Defying the tradition of prime ministers, Louis assumed sole, absolute administrative control of France.

23
00:02:51.400 --> 00:03:01.160
[George] He chose the sun as his royal emblem, representing Apollo, the god of light, order, and the arts, whose rays nourished and illuminated the world.

24
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:09.720
[George] Imbued with the doctrine of the divine right of kings, Louis believed he was God's lieutenant on earth, responsible only to the Almighty.

25
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:16.200
[George] Popular tradition attributed to him the immortal declaration El Etat, c'est moi, I am the state.

26
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:27.080
[George] For fifty-four years of personal rule, Louis worked eight hours a day at his council tables, transforming France into the most centralized, formidable nation-state in Europe.

27
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.320
[George] Louis X IV's daily administrative routine was relentless.

28
00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:43.480
[George] He rose at eight, spent mornings with his ministers reviewing foreign treaties, public finances and military reports, and insisted on approving all royal promotions and building contracts personally.

29
00:03:43.480 --> 00:04:02.320
[George] Colbert transformed France into an industrial and commercial powerhouse, creating state-subsidized royal manufacturers for Gobelin's tapestries, Saint-Gobin plate glass, and luxurious silks in Lyon, ensuring that French gold remained within the kingdom rather than flowing to foreign rivals.

30
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:18.000
[George] Determined to leave Paris behind and build a monumental stage worthy of his imperial vision, Louis Xivy chose Versailles, a mosquito-infested, swampy plateau twelve miles southwest of Paris, where his father had built a modest brick hunting lodge.

31
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:31.200
[George] For over four decades, employing architect Louis Laval, landscape architect André Lenotter, and painter Charles Le Brun, Louis transformed the wasteland into the grandest palace complex on planet Earth.

32
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:43.280
[George] Over 36,000 stonemasons, carpenters and laborers drained the swamps, moved entire hills, diverted rivers, and planted full-grown forests transported on timber carts.

33
00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:51.040
[George] The palace grew into a colossal Baroque monument capable of housing 10,000 courtiers, ministers, and servants.

34
00:04:51.040 --> 00:05:02.560
[George] The surrounding gardens spanned nearly 2,000 acres, adorned with fifty elaborate fountains, 1,400 jets of water, the Grand Canal, and thousands of marble statues.

35
00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:16.640
[George] To supply water for the fountains, French hydraulic engineers constructed the monumental Machine de Marley on the Seine River, a colossal mechanical wonder consisting of fourteen waterwheels pumping water up a 500-foot cliff.

36
00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:26.000
[George] Versailles was not merely a royal residence, it was a dazzling stone symbol of absolute order, where human intellect mastered nature itself.

37
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.000
[George] The construction of Versailles transformed French civil engineering.

38
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:41.240
[George] Thirty-six thousand horses and thousands of soldiers worked day and night, while the surrounding marshlands were drained to plant twenty miles of hornbeam hedges and thousands of potted orange trees.

39
00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:57.320
[George] The construction of Versailles required the labor of over thirty thousand stonemasons, carpenters, canal diggers and soldiers, working through brutal winters and malaria outbreaks to drain pestilential marshes and erect the most magnificent royal palace in human history.

40
00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:05.960
[George] The architectural jewel of Versailles was the breathtaking gallery des glaces, the Hall of Mirrors, completed in 1684.

41
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:22.200
[George] Stretching 240 feet along the palace's central terrace, the hall was lined with seventeen colossal arched windows overlooking the gardens, directly reflected by seventeen opposing arches containing five hundred and seventy-eight individual mirror panes.

42
00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:35.400
[George] At a time when mirror making was a jealously guarded Venetian monopoly, Louis's finance minister, Jean-Baptiste Colbert, smuggled Venetian glassmakers to France to establish royal glass manufactories.

43
00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.880
[George] Painted ceilings by Le Bruns celebrated Louis's military triumphs.

44
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:47.400
[George] Within these glittering halls, Louis executed his masterstroke of political domestic control, the ceremonial.

45
00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.880
[George] Louis transformed daily life into an intricate, sacred ritual.

46
00:06:51.880 --> 00:07:07.560
[George] Every morning at the lever, the royal rising, and every evening at the coucher, the royal retiring, hundreds of noblemen jostled, bowed, and competed for the supreme honor of handing the king his shirt, holding his candlestick, or tying his royal cravat.

47
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:20.640
[George] By compelling the aristocracy to live at Versailles under his watchful eye, consuming their wealth on lavish silks, wigs and gambling, Louis turned potential rebel warlords into dependent, harmless courtiers.

48
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:35.520
[George] The Hall of Mirrors served as the grand diplomatic showcase of the kingdom, where foreign ambassadors walked past hundreds of reflecting glass panes under gilded crystal chandeliers, awed by the optical brilliance that projected French royal majesty.

49
00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:51.200
[George] The king's daily routine was choreographed down to the second, turning the royal awakening, morning shaving, and evening bedtime into competitive court rituals where grand dukes and princes desperately vied for the honor of handing the king his silk shirt or candle.

50
00:07:51.200 --> 00:08:05.920
[George] Under Louis XIV's lavish royal patronage, France experienced an unprecedented cultural explosion that established French as the universal language of European diplomacy, literature, and intellectual discourse for two centuries.

51
00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:15.440
[George] Louis founded the Royal Academy of Painting and Sculpture, the Academy of Sciences, the Academy of Inscriptions, and the Royal Academy of Music.

52
00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:33.440
[George] He patronized the greatest artistic geniuses of the Baroque era, the playwright Molière, whose biding satirical comedies like Tartuffe and the Misanthrope were performed before the king, the tragic dramatist Jean Racine, and the composer Jean-Baptiste Lully, who pioneered French opera.

53
00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:39.360
[George] Louis himself was an accomplished dancer, performing in court ballets as the rising sun.

54
00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:54.720
[George] Meanwhile, Finance Minister Colbert enacted mercantilist policies, founding the French East India Company, establishing the Royal Tapestry Works at Gobelins, and building the French Navy into a force capable of rivaling England and the Netherlands.

55
00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:04.360
[George] French fashions, cuisine, classical architecture, and garden design were emulated by every royal court across the European continent, from Madrid to St.

56
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.240
[George] Petersburg.

57
00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:11.160
[George] Louis's patronage of playwrights and artists transformed France into the cultural capital of Europe.

58
00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:20.680
[George] Molière's theatrical troupe was officially titled The Troupe du Roy, performing comedies in the palace gardens before the royal family and high aristocracy.

59
00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:36.520
[George] The king's mistress, Madame de Montespan, became embroiled in the notorious affair of the poisons, an explosive court scandal involving alchemists, black masses, and toxic arsenic powders that threatened to implicate the highest echelons of the French nobility.

60
00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:41.560
[George] Yet beneath the golden splendor of the Sun King lay deep, tragic shadows.

61
00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:59.080
[George] Obsessed with absolute religious uniformity, Louis committed his greatest domestic blunder in October 1685 by issuing the Edict of Fontainebleau, which revoked the historic Edict of Nantes that had granted religious freedom to French Protestants, or Huguenots, for nearly a century.

62
00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:06.440
[George] Louis ordered Protestant churches destroyed, Protestant pastors exiled, and children baptized by force.

63
00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:24.200
[George] In response, over 200,000 skilled Huguenot merchants, silk weavers, bankers and craftsmen fled France, bringing their wealth, skills and industry to England, the Netherlands, Prussia, and the American colonies, inflicting an irreparable blow on the French economy.

64
00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:38.280
[George] Furthermore, Louis's relentless territorial ambition plunged France into decades of ruinous European wars, the War of Devolution, the Franco-Dutch War, the Nine Years' War, and the grueling war of the Spanish Succession.

65
00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:52.400
[George] While French borders expanded to include Alsace, French Comte and Flanders, the astronomical financial cost emptied the royal treasury, exhausted the population, and brought catastrophic famine to the French countryside.

66
00:10:52.400 --> 00:11:08.320
[George] The revocation of the Edict of Nantes cost France its most enterprising merchant families, as Huguenots carried secrets of silk manufacture to Spitalfields in London and clockmaking to Switzerland, while French armies fought exhausting wars on five continental fronts.

67
00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:23.360
[George] Voltaire later described the mysterious prisoner in his historical writings, noting that the captive was treated with extraordinary respect by his jailers, served delicious food on fine silver plate, and played the guitar in his isolated fortress cell.

68
00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:32.640
[George] Among the most intriguing, haunting mysteries of Louis XIV's reign was the solitary prisoner known to history as the Man in the Iron Mask.

69
00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:58.000
[George] Arrested around 1669 and held in maximum security under the personal supervision of royal jailer Bénin Dauvergny de Saint Mars in Pignorol, the fortress of Exils, the island of Saint Marguerite, and finally the Bastille in Paris, the prisoner was forced to wear a mask, historically of black velvet with steel hinges, whenever he was in the presence of guards, under penalty of instant death.

70
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:09.840
[George] When he died in the Bastille in November 1703, his clothing and mattress were burned, his cell walls scraped, and his metal dish melted down to erase all trace of his identity.

71
00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:16.160
[George] Voltaire famously suggested the prisoner was Louis XIV's older or twin illegitimate brother.

72
00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:22.720
[George] Modern historians suggest a royal valet named Eustache Dodger carrying dangerous diplomatic secrets.

73
00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:32.920
[George] On September 1, 1715, four days shy of his 77th birthday, Louis XIV died at Versailles after 72 years on the throne.

74
00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:41.800
[George] As his five-year-old great grandson, Louis XV was brought to his deathbed, the dying son king whispered words of humble confession.

75
00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:44.920
[George] Do not follow the bad example which I have set you.

76
00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:49.240
[George] I have often undertaken war too lightly and have sustained it for vanity.

77
00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:52.680
[George] Do not imitate me, but be a peaceful prince.

78
00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:59.000
[George] The golden age of Versailles was closing, setting the stage for the gathering storm of the French Revolution.

79
00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:14.840
[George] The mystery of the man in the iron mask became the subject of dozens of historical treatises, novels, and films, symbolizing the dark, secretive power of royal letters de Cachet that could consign an individual to lifelong nameless oblivion at the monarch's pleasure.

80
00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:34.520
[George] Despite the ruinous financial debts and peasant suffering caused by his endless wars, Louis XIV permanently established France as the cultural and political arbiter of Europe, leaving behind a legacy of classical grandeur, architectural majesty, and state institutions that outlived the Bourbon monarchy.

81
00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:44.280
[Rebecca] The Sun King built Versailles as an eternal temple of royal power, leaving behind a legacy of unmatched cultural splendor and ruinous debt.

82
00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:50.920
[Rebecca] Beyond the Pyrenees, the Kingdom of Spain was forging an empire of equal grandeur and fanaticism.

83
00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:55.640
[Rebecca] Next week we cross into the Iberian Peninsula with episode 216.

84
00:13:55.640 --> 00:14:00.280
[Rebecca] The Reconquista, Ferdinand and Isabella, and the Fall of Granada.

85
00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:11.400
[Rebecca] Seven centuries of holy war, the wedding that united Spain, the Spanish Inquisition of Torquomada, the tears of Boabdil at the Alhambra, and the fateful year of 1492.

86
00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.920
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

